<!-- YAM dual-arm station with crank_4310 grippers and a gantry-mounted Intel RealSense D405,
     generated from yam_station_crank_4310_d405.urdf (the source of truth) with the repo's standard
     URDF -> MJCF conversion. Kinematics only: no defaults, actuators, cameras or keyframe.

     `left_base` is the model root; `right_base` and `top_camera` hang off it as static
     bodies. Both arms share one <asset> mesh list; the arm meshes are referenced from
     arm/yam/v1/assets/ and the two merged camera meshes live in ../assets/, with d405_top.stl
     shared with the linear station.

     The `*_camera` and `top_camera` bodies are the optical frames (+Z along the optical axis,
     ROS/OpenCV convention) and each carries its camera assembly's merged mesh, so its <geom>
     needs no pos/quat: body frame, mesh frame and optical frame are one. See README.md for the
     extrinsics.

     `tcp_left` / `tcp_right` are the tool-centre frames, taken from
     gripper/crank_4310/crank_4310.xml's `grasp_site`: the flange turned 180 deg about X and carried
     out to the centre of the two claw points, 146.764 mm along the flange's -Z and 44.591 mm off
     its axis. That lateral offset is the geometry, not an error: each claw tapers to a point near
     one end of its jaw, so the two points meet well off the flange axis. Unlike the linear
     station's, this transform is gripper-specific. Sites are a MuJoCo concept, so the URDF has no
     counterpart. -->
